-- title:
--   Left ideals of supersingular endomorphism rings are kernel ideals
-- statement:
--   Let $\kappa$ be an algebraically closed field and $X_0$ a Weierstrass curve over $\kappa$ which is elliptic. Let $a,b\in\mathbb{Q}$, and let $\Lambda$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of the quaternion algebra $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ which is an order in the sense of the project's predicate [`QuaternionAlgebra.IsOrder`](def/QuaternionAlgebra_Order.html#L11): it contains $1$, is closed under multiplication, spans $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, and is finitely generated over $\mathbb{Z}$. Write $R$ for [`WeierstrassCurve.rationalEndSubring κ X₀`](def/WeierstrassCurve_RationalEnd.html#L32), the subring of the additive endomorphism ring of the group of points of the base change of $X_0$ to $\kappa$ generated by those additive endomorphisms that are zero or rationally represented. Assume given an injective ring homomorphism $\theta\colon R\to\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ whose range is exactly $\Lambda$. Let $L$ be a left ideal of $R$, let $n$ be a natural number with $n\neq 0$ in $\kappa$ and with the image of $n$ in $R$ lying in $L$, and let $r\in R$ be such that every point $P$ annihilated by all $l\in L$ satisfies $r(P)=0$. Then $r\in L$.
--
--   This is Waterhouse's theorem that a left ideal of the endomorphism ring of a supersingular elliptic curve containing an integer invertible in the base field is a kernel ideal, i.e. coincides with the full annihilator of the finite subgroup it cuts out; here supersingularity enters through the hypothesis that the rational endomorphism ring is isomorphic to an order in a rational quaternion algebra. It is used in the construction underlying the Cerednik–Drinfeld description, via [`CerednikDrinfeld.exists_kernelIdealSet_eq_star_smul_ofFiniteIdele`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.exists_kernelIdealSet_eq_star_smul_ofFiniteIdele).
